[?2004hroot@c5e45fe218ea:/app# cat >> README.md <<'MD'
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Resume / durability protocol
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The checkpoint's `last_id` is the **last committed** source ID; the next query
[?2004l[?2004h> starts strictly after it (IDs need not be contiguous). Each batch, or partial
[?2004l[?2004h> batch on SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP, is committed in this order:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Flush and fsync all affected shards, including shards closed by rotation.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Fsync the index directory and record committed shard names and byte sizes.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Write and fsync a temporary checkpoint, atomically replace the old checkpoint,
[?2004l[?2004h>    then fsync its directory.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> On restart, validate the committed files, truncate any bytes past those sizes,
[?2004l[?2004h> and remove shards absent from the checkpoint before continuing. Thus a SIGKILL
[?2004l[?2004h> between writing rows and publishing progress only replays uncommitted work;
[?2004l[?2004h> partial JSON/UTF-8 tails are discarded without parsing them. Missing/shortened
[?2004l[?2004h> committed files or malformed checkpoints fail loudly instead of skipping work.
[?2004l[?2004h> A completed rerun performs recovery but does not reindex documents.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Old checkpoints (without shard byte sizes) cannot be trusted: the old worker could
[?2004l[?2004h> publish unwritten rows. These are rebuilt once. As before, a missing checkpoint
[?2004l[?2004h> means a rebuild. The source corpus must remain fixed during a reindex; use
[?2004l[?2004h> `./jobs clean` to start a new generation after source changes. Run writers through
[?2004l[?2004h> `./jobs reindex` so the supervisor serializes access. Index row transformation is
[?2004l[?2004h> unchanged.
[?2004l[?2004h> MD
